        ":question/id": "9e9f5055-f5c3-5e44-823d-b027109ff59f",
        ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\ln(21 - x)$ at the point $(20,0).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$ f=\\ln u,\\,u=21 - x.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot(-1) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{21 - x}\\cdot(-1) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x - 21}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=20$ is\n\n$$m=\\left.(\\frac{1}{x - 21})\\right|_{x=20}=-1.$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(20,0)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(0) &=(-1)(x-(20)) \\\\ y &=20 - x.\\end{aligned}$$"
      },
